  <header class=3D"post-header">
    <h1 class=3D"post-title p-name" itemprop=3D"name headline">A Resource C=
ompiler with CMake and Standard C++</h1>
    <p class=3D"post-meta">
      <time class=3D"dt-published" datetime=3D"2017-01-21T00:00:00+00:00" i=
temprop=3D"datePublished">Jan 21, 2017
      </time></p>
  </header>

  <div class=3D"post-content e-content" itemprop=3D"articleBody">
    <p>I recently worked on a project which required that I serve a static =
web page
from an embedded C++ HTTP server. I could have gone the fairly simple route=
 of
installing the static page files alongside the executable and serving them
from the filesystem, but there was a catch: One of the requirements was ext=
reme
portability. This program should be a single executable file that can be mo=
ved
around and executed all by itself without having a bunch of files lying aro=
und.</p>

<p>I could have gone the truly hideous route of writing the entire static p=
age
inline as a string literal. I don=E2=80=99t think it requires much explaina=
tion on why
I didn=E2=80=99t want to do this. I opted to go with the single-page applic=
ation
approach: The interface itself is fairly simple. Still, I didn=E2=80=99t wa=
nt to have
something ugly and hard to maintain. I wanted to be able to work with all m=
y
favorite web tooling, Bower, Polymer, etc. I also wanted a half-decent edit=
ing
experience. It should be abundantly clear as to why an inline string litera=
l
approach would be completely unusable.</p>

<p>Of course, the best alternative is <em>resource compilation</em>. For an=
y unfamiliar
with the term: This is when you take arbitrary files and bake them into an
executable (or library), which you can then load and read from at runtime. =
It=E2=80=99s
a fairly common technique, but usually requires quite a bit of external too=
ling
and dependencies to get it up and running. There are a few common technique=
s:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Qt=E2=80=99s <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">qrc=
</code>: This is popular among Qt projects, and it works amazingly well. It
is tightly integrated with <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-ro=
uge">QFile</code> and <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">=
QDir</code>, so opening and reading files
that were compiled in as resources with <code class=3D"language-plaintext h=
ighlighter-rouge">qrc</code> is as simple as opening and
reading real files on disk. I=E2=80=99ve used it in the past to greate effe=
ct. The
big problem is that this requires dragging in the entire Qt ecosystem. Anyo=
ne
familiar with Qt knows that you can=E2=80=99t just pick-and-choose your fav=
orite parts
from Qt and just use those. It=E2=80=99s an all-or-nothing deal. That=E2=80=
=99s why Qt is
known as a <em>framework</em>, rather than a <em>library</em>. I didn=E2=80=
=99t want to pull in Qt
and all the things it entailed. I wanted an executable that was small and
easy to distribute, and Qt=E2=80=99s licensing would make that difficult. B=
esides
that, even if I were able to statically link Qt into the program, the binar=
y
size would probably explode (not to mention all the other libraries that Qt
depends on).</li>
  <li>Microsoft=E2=80=99s <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rou=
ge">rc</code>: This is common on Windows platforms, and is actually how
executables on Windows get their icons. I=E2=80=99m not as familiar with th=
is
technique, but I was unable to make use of it because I needed the program =
to
be cross-platform. This method just wouldn=E2=80=99t work.</li>
  <li><a href=3D"https://www.linuxjournal.com/content/embedding-file-execut=
able-aka-hello-world-version-5967">A pretty neat trick using <code class=3D=
"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">objcopy</code></a>:
I=E2=80=99ve tinkered with this in the past, but it=E2=80=99s somewhat impr=
actical because of
the weird implications with the linker and symbol names. Of course, it it a=
lso
only available when <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">ob=
jcopy</code> is also available, which again, makes it not
cross-platform. A no-go.</li>
  <li>Many, many, other toolset-specific techniques.</li>
</ul>

<p>I ended up inventing a CMake-based script which would compile the arbitr=
ary
files. I can=E2=80=99t show the exact script here (nor would I want to, it =
was a pretty
big hack), but I think I=E2=80=99d like to build something upon the same id=
eas:
Something that others my find usable and informative.</p>

<h1 id=3D"a-cmake-foundation">A CMake Foundation</h1>

<p>CMake has quite a few tricks up its sleeves that most users are complete=
ly
unaware of. I=E2=80=99ll be going over those in this post as we see what ki=
nd of magic
we can pull off. I=E2=80=99ll be building this CMake-based resource compile=
r as I write
this, so it will be learning experience for both of us. My goal is that all=
 of
this code should work with standard CMake and standard C++11, no more, no l=
ess.</p>

<p>Let=E2=80=99s begin!</p>

<h1 id=3D"the-process">The Process</h1>

<h2 id=3D"step-1-a-simple-test-case">Step 1: A Simple Test Case</h2>

<p>While I don=E2=80=99t want to do full-scale TDD, I want to write a littl=
e example that
I=E2=80=99d like to get working, and then actually implement. Here will be =
an example
C++ program:</p>

<div class=3D"language-c++ highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><pre=
 class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"cp">#include</span> <span class=
=3D"cpf">&lt;cmrc/cmrc.hpp&gt;</span><span class=3D"cp">
</span>
<span class=3D"cp">#include</span> <span class=3D"cpf">&lt;iostream&gt;</sp=
an><span class=3D"cp">
</span>
<span class=3D"kt">int</span> <span class=3D"nf">main</span><span class=3D"=
p">()</span> <span class=3D"p">{</span>
  <span class=3D"n">CMRC_INIT</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=
=3D"n">hello</span><span class=3D"p">);</span>
  <span class=3D"k">auto</span> <span class=3D"n">data</span> <span class=
=3D"o">=3D</span> <span class=3D"n">cmrc</span><span class=3D"o">::</span><=
span class=3D"n">open</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"s">"he=
llo.txt"</span><span class=3D"p">);</span>
  <span class=3D"n">std</span><span class=3D"o">::</span><span class=3D"n">=
cout</span> <span class=3D"o">&lt;&lt;</span> <span class=3D"n">std</span><=
span class=3D"o">::</span><span class=3D"n">string</span><span class=3D"p">=
(</span><span class=3D"n">data</span><span class=3D"p">.</span><span class=
=3D"n">begin</span><span class=3D"p">(),</span> <span class=3D"n">data</spa=
n><span class=3D"p">.</span><span class=3D"n">end</span><span class=3D"p">(=
))</span> <span class=3D"o">&lt;&lt;</span> <span class=3D"sc">'\n'</span><=
span class=3D"p">;</span>
<span class=3D"p">}</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Here=E2=80=99s my idea:</p>

<ol>
  <li><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">cmrc/cmrc.hpp</c=
ode> will be a header file with the resource-file loading library.</li>
  <li><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">CMRC_INIT</code>=
 will initialize the resource library. This will be required, I
believe. We=E2=80=99ll discuss it a bit more later.</li>
  <li><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">cmrc::open</code=
> will open a compiled-in resource by filename. What type does it
return? I=E2=80=99m not sure yet=E2=80=A6 But it should have a <code class=
=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">begin()</code> and <code class=3D=
"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">end()</code>
returning two RandomAccessIterators.</li>
</ol>

<p>That=E2=80=99s it! Fairly simple, and I=E2=80=99m hoping that upon this =
one could build more
complex and useful abstractions.</p>

<p>Compiling the file would be simple. Here=E2=80=99s the <code class=3D"la=
nguage-plaintext highlighter-rouge">CMakeLists.txt</code> I=E2=80=99d like =
to
have:</p>

<div class=3D"language-cmake highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><p=
re class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"nb">cmake_minimum_required</spa=
n><span class=3D"p">(</span>VERSION 3.6<span class=3D"p">)</span>
<span class=3D"nb">project</span><span class=3D"p">(</span>CMRCExample<span=
 class=3D"p">)</span>

<span class=3D"nb">include</span><span class=3D"p">(</span>CMakeRC.cmake<sp=
an class=3D"p">)</span>

<span class=3D"nf">cmrc_add_resource_library</span><span class=3D"p">(</spa=
n>hello hello.txt<span class=3D"p">)</span>
<span class=3D"nb">add_executable</span><span class=3D"p">(</span>main main=
.cpp<span class=3D"p">)</span>
<span class=3D"nb">target_link_libraries</span><span class=3D"p">(</span>ma=
in PRIVATE hello<span class=3D"p">)</span>

<span class=3D"nb">include</span><span class=3D"p">(</span>CTest<span class=
=3D"p">)</span>
<span class=3D"nb">enable_testing</span><span class=3D"p">()</span>
<span class=3D"nb">add_test</span><span class=3D"p">(</span>main main<span =
class=3D"p">)</span>
<span class=3D"nb">set_property</span><span class=3D"p">(</span>TEST main P=
ROPERTY PASS_REGULAR_EXPRESSION <span class=3D"s2">"Hello, world!"</span><s=
pan class=3D"p">)</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Here=E2=80=99s the idea here:</p>

<ol>
  <li><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">CMakeRC.cmake</c=
ode> is the script that we=E2=80=99ll be going over in this post. It will
be doing most of the heavy lifting.</li>
  <li><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">cmrc_add_resourc=
e_library</code> does a bunch of magic and eventually calls
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">add_library</code> to =
generate a new target with the given resource files compiled
in.</li>
  <li><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">target_link_libr=
aries</code> called with the resource library will make the
resources defined in the linked library available to the linkee.</li>
  <li>The simple test case should output <code class=3D"language-plaintext =
highlighter-rouge">Hello, world!</code>, so we set that as the
expected output for CTest to check for us.</li>
</ol>

<p>Of course, we need an example resource file to compile. That will be
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">hello.txt</code>, whic=
h contains the text <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">He=
llo, world!</code>. We should expect that to
be the output of the program.</p>

<h2 id=3D"step-2-getting-the-test-case-to-build">Step 2: Getting the Test C=
ase to Build</h2>

<p>Of course, trying to build the test case won=E2=80=99t even configure. W=
e can get it to
at least configure by adding a <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighte=
r-rouge">CMakeRC.cmake</code> file like this:</p>

<div class=3D"language-cmake highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><p=
re class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"nb">function</span><span class=
=3D"p">(</span>cmrc_add_resource_library name<span class=3D"p">)</span>
    <span class=3D"nb">add_library</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span cl=
ass=3D"si">${</span><span class=3D"nv">name</span><span class=3D"si">}</spa=
n> INTERFACE<span class=3D"p">)</span>
<span class=3D"nb">endfunction</span><span class=3D"p">()</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>We=E2=80=99ll see it fail to build immediately as it cannot find <code c=
lass=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">cmrc.hpp</code>. We must
generate this file! We can do that when <code class=3D"language-plaintext h=
ighlighter-rouge">CMakeRC.cmake</code> is first included. Let=E2=80=99s
add some basic content at the top of the module:</p>

<div class=3D"language-cmake highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><p=
re class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"nb">if</span><span class=3D"p">=
(</span>COMMAND cmrc_add_resource_library<span class=3D"p">)</span>
    <span class=3D"c1"># CMakeRC has already been included! Don't do anythi=
ng</span>
    <span class=3D"nb">return</span><span class=3D"p">()</span>
<span class=3D"nb">endif</span><span class=3D"p">()</span>

<span class=3D"nb">set</span><span class=3D"p">(</span>this_script <span cl=
ass=3D"s2">"</span><span class=3D"si">${</span><span class=3D"nv">CMAKE_CUR=
RENT_LIST_FILE</span><span class=3D"si">}</span><span class=3D"s2">"</span>=
<span class=3D"p">)</span>

<span class=3D"nb">set</span><span class=3D"p">(</span>CMRC_INCLUDE_DIR <sp=
an class=3D"s2">"</span><span class=3D"si">${</span><span class=3D"nv">CMAK=
E_BINARY_DIR</span><span class=3D"si">}</span><span class=3D"s2">/_cmrc/inc=
lude"</span> CACHE INTERNAL <span class=3D"s2">"Directory for CMakeRC inclu=
de files"</span><span class=3D"p">)</span>
<span class=3D"c1"># Let's generate the primary include file</span>
<span class=3D"nb">file</span><span class=3D"p">(</span>MAKE_DIRECTORY <spa=
n class=3D"s2">"</span><span class=3D"si">${</span><span class=3D"nv">CMRC_=
INCLUDE_DIR</span><span class=3D"si">}</span><span class=3D"s2">/cmrc"</spa=
n><span class=3D"p">)</span>
<span class=3D"nb">file</span><span class=3D"p">(</span>WRITE <span class=
=3D"s2">"</span><span class=3D"si">${</span><span class=3D"nv">CMRC_INCLUDE=
_DIR</span><span class=3D"si">}</span><span class=3D"s2">/cmrc/cmrc.hpp"</s=
pan> <span class=3D"s2">[=3D=3D[
#ifndef CMRC_CMRC_HPP_INCLUDED
#define CMRC_CMRC_HPP_INCLUDED

#include &lt;string&gt;

