an class=3D"s2">/</span><span class=3D"si">${</span><span class=3D"nv">name=
</span><span class=3D"si">}</span><span class=3D"s2">-intermediate/</span><=
span class=3D"si">${</span><span class=3D"nv">relpath</span><span class=3D"=
si">}</span><span class=3D"s2">.cpp"</span> ABSOLUTE<span class=3D"p">)</sp=
an>
        <span class=3D"c1"># Generate the rule for the intermediate source =
file</span>
        <span class=3D"nf">_cmrc_generate_intermediate_cpp</span><span clas=
s=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"s2">"</span><span class=3D"si">${</span><spa=
n class=3D"nv">abspath</span><span class=3D"si">}</span><span class=3D"s2">=
"</span> <span class=3D"s2">"</span><span class=3D"si">${</span><span class=
=3D"nv">abs_input</span><span class=3D"si">}</span><span class=3D"s2">"</sp=
an><span class=3D"p">)</span>
        <span class=3D"nb">list</span><span class=3D"p">(</span>APPEND sour=
ces <span class=3D"s2">"</span><span class=3D"si">${</span><span class=3D"n=
v">abspath</span><span class=3D"si">}</span><span class=3D"s2">"</span><spa=
n class=3D"p">)</span>
    <span class=3D"nb">endforeach</span><span class=3D"p">()</span>
    <span class=3D"c1"># Generate the actual static library. Each source fi=
le is just a single file</span>
    <span class=3D"c1"># with a character array compiled in containing the =
contents of the</span>
    <span class=3D"c1"># corresponding resource file.</span>
    <span class=3D"nb">add_library</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span cl=
ass=3D"si">${</span><span class=3D"nv">name</span><span class=3D"si">}</spa=
n> STATIC <span class=3D"si">${</span><span class=3D"nv">sources</span><spa=
n class=3D"si">}</span><span class=3D"p">)</span>
    <span class=3D"nb">target_link_libraries</span><span class=3D"p">(</spa=
n><span class=3D"si">${</span><span class=3D"nv">name</span><span class=3D"=
si">}</span> PUBLIC cmrc::base<span class=3D"p">)</span>
<span class=3D"nb">endfunction</span><span class=3D"p">()</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Currently, we error if the resource it outside of the source directory. =
That=E2=80=99s
pretty bad, but we=E2=80=99ll get to that later, as it requires a bit of tr=
ickery to get
right.</p>

<p>If you try to build now, you=E2=80=99ll see an error about <code class=
=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">add_library</code> being
un-scriptable. This is because <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighte=
r-rouge">_cmrc_generate_intermediate_cpp</code> uses the
existing <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">CMakeRC.cmake=
</code> as the script that will generate the intermediate
C++ file. When using <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">-=
P</code> mode to CMake, we cannot use some commands, including
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">add_library</code> (af=
ter all, such a thing doesn=E2=80=99t make sense). This is the
purpose of the <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">_CMRC_G=
ENERATE_MODE</code> flag I mentioned earlier. At the top of
the <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">CMakeRC.cmake</cod=
e> script, we=E2=80=99ll add a short-circuit to do something different
if this flag is set:</p>

<div class=3D"language-cmake highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><p=
re class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"nb">if</span><span class=3D"p">=
(</span>_CMRC_GENERATE_MODE<span class=3D"p">)</span>
    <span class=3D"nb">message</span><span class=3D"p">(</span>STATUS <span=
 class=3D"s2">"Generating file </span><span class=3D"si">${</span><span cla=
ss=3D"nv">OUTPUT_FILE</span><span class=3D"si">}</span><span class=3D"s2">"=
</span><span class=3D"p">)</span>
    <span class=3D"nb">return</span><span class=3D"p">()</span>
<span class=3D"nb">endif</span><span class=3D"p">()</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>If I run the build now, we=E2=80=99ll see <code class=3D"language-plaint=
ext highlighter-rouge">Generating file &lt;some-path&gt;/hello.txt.cpp</cod=
e>,
followed by an immediate compiler error because it tries to compile that C+=
+
file, but we never really generated it. So, how do we generate it?</p>

<p>When I was initially thinking about how to solve this resource-compiling
problem, the one thing that made me immediately think that CMake was up to =
the
task was one very specific and rarely used CMake feature: <code class=3D"la=
nguage-plaintext highlighter-rouge">file(READ HEX)</code>. Yep,
CMake=E2=80=99s <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">file(R=
EAD)</code> command lets you read in the bytes of a file as a sequence
of hexadecimal digits, just by using the <code class=3D"language-plaintext =
highlighter-rouge">HEX</code> keyword on the end. And
what can we do with those digits? Well, we can use them to generate charact=
er
literals, of course! Here=E2=80=99s a simple example:</p>

<div class=3D"language-cmake highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><p=
re class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"c1"># Read in the digits</span>
<span class=3D"nb">file</span><span class=3D"p">(</span>READ <span class=3D=
"s2">"</span><span class=3D"si">${</span><span class=3D"nv">INPUT_FILE</spa=
n><span class=3D"si">}</span><span class=3D"s2">"</span> bytes HEX<span cla=
ss=3D"p">)</span>
<span class=3D"c1"># Forat each pair into a character literal</span>
<span class=3D"nb">string</span><span class=3D"p">(</span>REGEX REPLACE <sp=
an class=3D"s2">"(..)"</span> <span class=3D"s2">"'</span><span class=3D"se=
">\\\\</span><span class=3D"s2">x</span><span class=3D"se">\\</span><span c=
lass=3D"s2">1', "</span> chars <span class=3D"s2">"</span><span class=3D"si=
">${</span><span class=3D"nv">bytes</span><span class=3D"si">}</span><span =
class=3D"s2">"</span><span class=3D"p">)</span>
<span class=3D"c1"># Just write it to stdout for this example</span>
<span class=3D"nb">message</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"s=
2">"</span><span class=3D"si">${</span><span class=3D"nv">chars</span><span=
 class=3D"si">}</span><span class=3D"s2">"</span><span class=3D"p">)</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>This example stands entirely on its own, none of the other code is neede=
d.
Execute it like so:</p>

<div class=3D"language-sh highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><pre =
class=3D"highlight"><code>cmake <span class=3D"nt">-DINPUT_FILE</span><span=
 class=3D"o">=3D</span>hello.txt <span class=3D"nt">-P</span> CMakeRC.cmake
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>You=E2=80=99ll see the contents of the file printed to standard out, but=
 as a
comma-separated list of character literals:</p>

<div class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight=
"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code>'\x48', '\x65', '\x6c', '\x6c', '\x6f', '\=
x2c', '\x20', '\x77', '\x6f', '\x72', '\x6c', '\x64', '\x21',
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Integrating this into our existing <code class=3D"language-plaintext hig=
hlighter-rouge">CMakeRC.cmake</code> is very simple:</p>

<div class=3D"language-cmake highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><p=
re class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"nb">if</span><span class=3D"p">=
(</span>_CMRC_GENERATE_MODE<span class=3D"p">)</span>
    <span class=3D"c1"># Read in the digits</span>
    <span class=3D"nb">file</span><span class=3D"p">(</span>READ <span clas=
s=3D"s2">"</span><span class=3D"si">${</span><span class=3D"nv">INPUT_FILE<=
/span><span class=3D"si">}</span><span class=3D"s2">"</span> bytes HEX<span=
